Hello! If you're here, you're interested in understanding how ddhx works
internally.

This document explains:
- How the codebase is organized
- The program's execution flow
- How the Piece Table + Red-Black tree backend works
- The command-based undo/redo system

For user-facing documentation, see the main README in the project root.

********************************************************************************
* SOURCE STRUCTURE
********************************************************************************

The folder structure is pretty straight-forward:
- ddhx/editor/*
  Contains editor backends. Each provide an implementation of a document
  editor.
- ddhx/document/*
  Source documents. Often representing a specific media, such as a file, a disk
  a process, and other physical representation of a source document.
  Currently, only saving to files are supported, it is planned that each
  document will have different strategies to save modifications.
- ddhx/*
  Other files that compliments the engine.
- os/*
  Operation system interfaces.
- *
  TUI front-end files.

********************************************************************************
* PROGRAM CONTROL FLOW
********************************************************************************

      START
    +--------+ - Command-line parsing, init logging
    | main.d | - Initialize default keys, commands
    +--------+ - Find and parse configuration
        |      - Initialize document editor, open document
        v
    +--------+ - Setup terminal and session
    | view.d | - Loop (read input, modify state, update)
    +--------+ - menu (prompt) system, command implementation
        |      - Renders text/data to buffer
        v
+---------------+ - Manage terminal features
| os/terminal.d | - readkey, readline implementation
+---------------+ - Translate key names to key values
        |
        v
    + ~ ~ ~ ~+
    | screen |
    +~ ~ ~ ~ +

********************************************************************************
* COMMAND LINE
********************************************************************************

This section is about typing commands at the ':' prompt and in .ddhxrc. The
COMMANDS section further down is about the undo history, which is unrelated.

A typed line goes through two steps:

  utils.arguments()   Splits the line into arguments, applying quoting and C
                      escapes.
  patterns.pattern()  Turns one argument into a needle, applying prefixes and
                      wildcards.

*
* QUOTING RULES
*

Quoting style selects how the text is read, the same way it does in a
programming language. The two quotes buy two independent things, and neither of
them buys the other's:

                    protects whitespace    escape sequences
    text            no                     no
    'text'          yes                    no
    "text"          yes                    yes

So text is raw unless it is double quoted, which makes it a C string literal:

    find utf8:C:\Users              a path, typed as a path
    find utf8:'C:\Program Files'    a path with a space, quoted for the space
    find utf8:"\x1b[0m"             an escape sequence, opted into

From there:

1. Adjacent runs concatenate, so the forms mix freely within one argument. That
   is a plain concatenation of literals, as in any language that has more than
   one kind of them:

       find utf8:'C:\Users'"\0"    a raw path with a NUL after it
       find utf8:'a'"\t"'b'        tab between two raw runs

   A prefix may sit inside or outside the quotes: "utf8:abc" and utf8:"abc"
   are the same thing.

2. There is no escape outside of double quotes, not even \" and \'. A quote is
   written by using the other kind: "'" for an apostrophe, '"' for a double
   quote. The POSIX 'it'\''s' idiom does not apply, since the backslash is data
   here; it is 'it'"'"'s' instead.

3. An unknown escape is an error. "C:\dir" names \d and stops;
   it is written 'C:\dir' or "C:\\dir".

4. An unterminated quote is an error.

5. Empty quotes produce no argument rather than an empty one. `find ''` sees
   nothing at all.

6. Wildcards are a product of their own and not part of the quoting process.

Escapes are resolved where the quotes are read, in the same pass, so an
argument is bytes rather than text:

  utils.Argument    immutable(ubyte)[] data, quotes removed and escapes
                    resolved, plus a .text() that validates UTF-8 and throws
                    if the bytes are not.

One pass also means the escape rules cannot differ per command, since they are
applied before a command is chosen. "\xff" resolves to one byte for find and
for diff-file alike, whether or not the command can do anything with it.

*
* PREFIXES
*

Step 2 is prefixes. The split that organizes them is not the radix, it is byte
string versus scalar:

    BYTE STRING     already a sequence of bytes, so it is taken as written and
                    has neither a width nor an endian to ask about
    SCALAR          a value that still has to be encoded, so it has both

    x: 0x                 bytes, two hex digits each
    utf8:                 bytes, text as typed
    utf16: utf32:         text, transcoded, one scalar per code unit
    utf8bom: utf16bom:    likewise, preceded by a byte order mark
    utf32bom:
    x8: x16: x32: x64:    scalar, unsigned, that many bytes (bit indicator)
    u8: u16: u32: u64:    likewise, decimal
    o8: o16: o32: o64:    likewise, octal
    i8: i16: i32: i64:    likewise, signed, two's complement
    f32: f64:             likewise, IEEE-754, that many bytes
    f80:                  likewise, x87's 80-bit extended, ten bytes

Hexadecimal is the only radix on both sides, because two digits are exactly one
byte. Decimal and octal do not tile bytes, so they are only ever scalars, and a
scalar always names its width: the bare d:, o:, and 0o are gone, decimal being
u8:..u64: unsigned and i8:..i64: signed. 0x survives the same cut only because
it is a byte string, like the x: it abbreviates.

Text names its encoding rather than taking the charset setting, which is
what 's:' was going to do. Endian works as a setting because a file picks one
and the toggle is cheap; a character set is the text column's setting, so a
needle sourced from it changes meaning with state that is not in the line and
searching EBCDIC while reading ASCII would mean flipping it twice per search.

Additionally, a s: string prefix says nothing about its encoding leaves the
bytes it produces up to the character set setting, and an encoding is a
property of the file being looked at. That's why 'utf8:' was chosen over 's:':
It is both D's native format and what the readline implementation returns.
It belongs on the needle. utf16: and utf32: followed, and neither changes what
utf8: means. string/wstring/dstring terms only used in D.

utf8: is the pass-through, since the line hands over UTF-8 already, and it is
byte transparent: utf8:"\xff" is a byte nobody claimed was a character, which
is what makes the escapes worth having. utf16: and utf32: transcode, so they
need text to work with and refuse anything that is not UTF-8, including a lone
surrogate, rather than widening a byte to a code unit (that would be Latin-1
wearing a UTF-16 name). x16: is how an unpaired D800 is searched for.

A code unit is a scalar like any other, so it is ordered by the endian setting
and there is no utf16le:/utf16be: pair to keep in step with it. The pair is
ordered a half at a time, so U+10000 is 00 D8 00 DC little endian and not four
bytes reversed, which is what a file holds.

No mark is emitted unless one is asked for by name, since a marked file has it
at the front and not before every match. The '*bom:' forms are what asks: they
are the mark plus the text, which is what the front of such a file holds, so
utf8bom:hi is EF BB BF 68 69 and utf16bom:hi is FF FE 68 00 69 00. The mark is
U+FEFF in the encoding named, so the wide two are ordered by the endian setting
along with the text behind them, and nothing else about the argument changes:
utf8bom: is still byte transparent and utf16bom: still refuses what is not text.

None of the three reach anywhere the line could not already reach, and each
earns its place anyway, a prefix being cheap next to what it can save someone
typing. utf8bom: saves recognizing EF BB BF, the only mark that does not read
as U+FEFF on sight. utf16bom: and utf32bom: save more than that: spelled by
hand a mark is a byte string, x:fffe, and a byte string does not follow the
endian setting, so it is on the reader to pick FF FE or FE FF and to pick again
whenever the setting moves. x16:feff is the spelling that does follow, which is
one more thing to know rather than fewer. The prefix cannot get it wrong: the
mark is U+FEFF in the encoding named, ordered by the same setting as the text
behind it, so utf16bom:hi is right in both orders and stays right.

A mark alone is still x:efbbbf or x16:feff, since a prefix with nothing after
it is a typo here as everywhere else.

Floats are named by their width in bits like everything else, so f32: and f64:
rather than 'float:' and 'double:', and what they encode is the bit pattern the
value occupies in the file, not the digits it was typed as. They are the one
scalar whose sign is not the prefix's business: there is no unsigned float to
tell them apart from, so the literal keeps its own '-', and "nan" and "inf" are
accepted since neither is memorable as hex. A literal that only fits once
rounded to infinity (f32:1e39) is an error, the same as any other value too
large for the width asked for.

'f80:' is the odd one, being x87's 80-bit extended rather than an IEEE-754
binary format: ten bytes, a 64-bit significand and then the sign and exponent,
with the integer bit stored rather than implied. It is named by width like the
others even though its width is not a power of two, since that is what a file
holds and what the x86 ABIs call long double.

All three take named constants as well as digits, since the point of typing a
float instead of its bytes is that its bytes are not recognizable:

    inf                           one encoding, the format says so
    nan qnan snan snan_hi         the NaNs, below
    indefinite                    ...including Intel's
    pi e tau sqrt2 phi            maths, as compiled into binaries
    max min epsilon denorm_min    the format's edges, as used for sentinels

A name is the whole literal, sign aside, so nothing can shadow a number: the 'e'
in "1e5" is an exponent and "e" alone is the constant, "f32:pi2" is an error
rather than half a match, and matching is case insensitive as std.conv already
is for nan and inf. Each width gets its own value, which matters for the four
that are properties of the format rather than numbers: f32:max is the largest
binary32, not the largest binary64 narrowed down, which would be infinity.

At f80: the constants are written out as bit patterns rather than computed,
because there is no host type to compute them in (below), and the mathematical
ones carry all 64 bits: f80:pi is the ten bytes FLDPI loads. The unittest
re-derives every one of them from decimal digits, so the table is checked and
not merely trusted.

The NaNs are named in IEEE's own words, quiet and signaling, rather than in
invented ones. A NaN is any nonzero mantissa, so which one is a choice:

    nan  qnan       7FC00000    quiet, the standard's recommended bit
    snan            7F800001    signaling, so the quiet bit clear, payload 1
    snan_hi         7FA00000    signaling, payload's high bit
    indefinite      FFC00000    Intel's name for the negative quiet NaN

The quiet bit is a recommendation of IEEE-754 rather than a rule, and one that
x86, ARM, SPARC, POWER and RISC-V follow while MIPS before r6 and PA-RISC invert
it. ddhx spells the recommended one everywhere, being the format's and not the
host's:

    binary32   0 11111111 1 0000000000000000000000    nan
               0 11111111 0 1000000000000000000000    snan_hi
               0 11111111 0 0000000000000000000001    snan
                 exponent ^ quiet bit

The "hi" in snan_hi is the high bit of the payload, which is the bit just under
the quiet bit and so the highest one a signaling NaN may set without going
quiet. It is named for that bit rather than for a toolchain, though it is what
GCC and glibc's SNANF emit; they pick it because a payload there keeps its
position relative to the quiet bit when the width changes, where a payload of 1
does not. 7FA00000 widens to 7FFC000000000000 with its shape intact, while
7F800001 widens to 7FF8000020000000. Any other payload is a byte string, which
is what x32: is for.

"indefinite" is a spelling of -qnan rather than a fifth value, and it earns a
name by being the NaN most likely to be sitting in a file: x87 and SSE produce
it for 0.0/0.0 and the other invalid operations, where ARM produces the positive
one. Which is also why a NaN's sign is honoured here even though std.conv drops
it: -nan and indefinite have to be sayable, or the common case is unreachable.

These four are encoded as bit patterns and never as float values, deliberately.
A float holding a signaling NaN is not safe to pass around, since x87 quiets one
on the way through a register, so an snan carried as a value would arrive as a
qnan on a 32-bit x86 build.

f32: is IEEE-754 binary32 and f64: binary64, on every host. Not "whatever this
machine calls a float": a needle describes bytes in a file, and a file does not
change format because of the machine reading it. The named constants are taken
from host floats, which happen to be those formats, and a static assert in
floating() checks that rather than assuming it; the NaNs above are spelled out
rather than borrowed, since IEEE-754 pins infinity exactly but allows any
nonzero mantissa for a NaN and hosts disagree on which one they hand out.

Literals do not touch a host float at any width. decimal() converts them in
integer arithmetic: a literal is digits times a power of ten, which is an exact
rational, so it is built as one and divided to a significand of the width asked
for, rounded to nearest with ties to even as IEEE-754 asks. Three things make
that worth the arithmetic:

  - std.conv accumulates in `real`, which is x87 extended on x86 and a plain
    double on AArch64. On the second kind of host the intermediate rounding
    survives into the answer, so "3.14159265358979323846" parsed as an f64
    landed an ULP off the pi constant. A needle whose bytes depend on the
    machine that typed it is not a needle, and this one shipped: it was an
    aarch64 CI failure, not a hypothetical.
  - Rounding to a wide format and then to a narrow one is not rounding to the
    narrow one. f32: used to convert through a double, so a literal a hair
    above the tie between 1.0 and the float after it rounded up on the way to
    the double, landed exactly on the tie, and then took the even neighbour:
    3F800000 where the digits say 3F800001.
  - f80: has no host type to borrow in the first place, and a double is eleven
    bits short of it besides (the twenty digits of pi widen to 4000
    C90FDAA22168C000, where the value they spell is 4000 C90FDAA22168C235,
    which is what FLDPI loads). Doing it exactly also brings the format's own
    exponent range along, and range is half of why a file holds one of these
    (f80:1e400 has no double to pass through).

The three formats differ only in significand bits, bias, and exponent range, so
they share one conversion and differ at packing time, where binary32 and
binary64 imply the leading significand bit and x87 stores it. The unittests pin
literals whose correctly rounded values are known from glibc's strtod and
strtof, including both textbook hard cases (1e23 and 2.2250738585072011e-308)
and the double-rounding one above.

The rule underneath both sides is that the prefix decides the length and the
value never does. A byte string is as long as what was typed, a scalar is as
long as its width says, and nothing is measured by how large a number happens
to be.

Endianness is not in the prefix either. Scalars are encoded with the `endian`
setting, the same one the inspector uses and Alt+E flips. An argument without a
prefix continues the previous one, width included, so `find u16:1 2` is two
16-bit values.

*
* EXPECTATIONS
*

These are the cases the design is pinned to. The executable copies live in the
unittests of utils.d (splitting and escapes alone), patterns.d (prefixes alone),
and the unittest at the end of patterns.d (both, by typed command line).

  TYPED                             NEEDLE (or result)
  find utf8:a\tb                    'a' 5C 't' 'b'      raw
  find utf8:'a\tb'                  'a' 5C 't' 'b'      raw, quotes buy whitespace
  find utf8:"a\tb"                  'a' 09 'b'          C string
  find utf8:C:\Users                C:\Users            no doubling, no quoting
  find utf8:'C:\Example Space\2'    C:\Example Space\2  quoted only for the space
  find utf8:"C:\\Example Space\\2"  C:\Example Space\2  C string, so doubled
  find utf8:C:\                     C:\                 needle ending in backslash
  find utf8:'C:\'                   C:\                 ...in any of the three
  find utf8:"C:\\"                  C:\                    quoting forms
  find utf8:C:\\Users               C:\\Users           raw, so two is two
  find utf8:\x1b[0m                 5C 'x' '1' 'b' ...  raw, so not an escape
  find utf8:"\x1b[0m"               1B '[' '0' 'm'      C string, so it is
  find utf8:"C:\Users"              error, unknown escape \U
  find utf8:"abc\"                  error, unterminated quote
  find utf8:'C:\Users'"\0"          C:\Users 00         concatenated per run
  find utf8:C:\dir"a b"             C:\dira b           ...same, no rule broken
  find utf8:'it'"'"'s'              it's                a quote needs no escape
  find utf16:hi                     68 00 69 00         code units, per endian
  find utf32:hi                     68 00 00 00 69 00 00 00
  find utf16:<U+1F600>              3D D8 00 DE         a pair, ordered by half
  find utf8:"\xff"                  FF                  transparent, so a byte
  find utf16:"\xff"                 error, nothing to transcode
  find utf16le:hi                   error, byte order is the endian setting's
  find utf8bom:hi                   EF BB BF 68 69      the mark, then the text
  find utf16bom:hi                  FF FE 68 00 69 00   ...ordered like the text
  find utf16bom:a b                 FF FE 61 00 62 00   one needle, one mark
  find utf8bom:                     error, a mark alone is x:efbbbf
  find x:1122                       11 22               bytes, as written
  find x16:1122                     22 11               a value, so encoded
  find x:0001                       00 01               no leading zero eaten
  find x:fff                        error, half a byte on the end
  find u16:255                      ff 00               length is the prefix's
  find u16:256                      00 01                  ...not the value's
  find u8:256                       error, too large for its width
  find d:255                        error, unknown prefix; d: named no width
  find i16:-1                       ff ff               two's complement
  find u16:-1                       error, only i* takes a sign
  find u8:max                       ff                  the width's own edge
  find i8:max                       7f                  ...per signedness
  find i8:min                       80                     ...and it is signed
  find u8:min                       00                  unsigned starts at zero
  find i8:-max                      81                  a name takes a sign
  find i8:-min                      error, +128 is the value the width lacks
  find u64:max                      ff ff ff ff ff ff ff ff   beats retyping it
  find u8:maximum                   error, a name is the whole literal
  find f32:1.0                      00 00 80 3F         IEEE-754, not the digits
  find f64:1.0                      00 00 00 00 00 00 F0 3F
  find f32:nan                      00 00 C0 7F         the quiet one, always
  find f32:-nan                     00 00 C0 FF         a NaN keeps its sign
  find f32:indefinite               00 00 C0 FF         ...which x86 hands out
  find f32:snan                     01 00 80 7F         signaling, payload 1
  find f32:pi                       DB 0F 49 40         a name, not digits
  find f32:-pi                      DB 0F 49 C0         ...and it takes a sign
  find f32:max                      FF FF 7F 7F         the format's own edge
  find f32:pi2                      error, a name is the whole literal
  find f32:1e39                     error, rounds to infinity at that width
  find f80:1.0                      00 00 00 00 00 00 00 80 FF 3F    ten bytes
  find f80:pi                       35 C2 68 21 A2 DA 0F C9 00 40    ditto
  find f80:3.14159265358979323846   ...the same, all 64 bits of it.  bit and all
  find f80:1e400                    E6 F9 9F CB C8 3F 76 DA 2F 45    no double
  find f80:1e4933                   error, past the largest finite value
  find x:00 * x:FF                  00 <any> FF
  find u16:1 2                      01 00 02 00         prefix carries, width too
  find utf8:*                       '*'                 prefixed, so data
  find '*'                          <any run>           quoting does not matter
  find "quoted"                     error, unknown prefix; quotes are not a type
  find ''                           no argument at all, can't search nothing
  find utf8:'abc                    error, unterminated quote
  diff-file 'C:\dump.bin'           opens C:\dump.bin   raw, so no escaping
  diff-file "C:\\dump.bin"          opens C:\dump.bin   C string, so doubled
  diff-file "C:\dump.bin"           error, unknown escape \d
  save-as "dump\x2Etxt"             saves dump.txt      escapes are not pattern-only
  save-as "\xff"                    error, argument is not valid UTF-8 text

*
* RATIONALE
*

The core idea of a hex editor is to manipulate data with precision. For that,
it needs to be precise, and it needs to be constant in this precision.

None of the above is forced. Why each of them, once:

- An argument is bytes because escapes are resolved before a command sees it,
  and resolving escapes rules out text as the type. "\0" and "\xff" are not
  valid UTF-8, and an argument flows into every command, into status messages,
  and into the logger. A command that wants text asks for it and takes the
  exception; a command that wants bytes takes the bytes and never has to care.
  Filenames on POSIX are byte strings anyway, so this is not only a pattern's
  problem. This also allows for commands that work with data to see data as-is.

- Escapes resolve in one pass, while the quotes are being read, because that is
  the only place that still knows which quote a byte came out of. Carrying the
  quoting onward instead was tried, first as one flag per argument, which has to
  pick a meaning for utf8:C:\dir"a b" or refuse it, then as a flag per span,
  which works but makes every consumer walk spans and offset around its prefix.
  Resolving on the spot deletes both and benefits all commands.

- One pass is also why the escape rules cannot differ per command: the line is
  read before a command is chosen. What that costs is diff-file "C:\dir", which
  is an unknown escape rather than a path.

- An unknown escape is an error. One pass means one policy for every command,
  so it may as well be the obvious one. A compiler stops on invalid escapes,
  because it's clear it's an invalid combination of text for the special
  double-quote text.

- Unquoted text is raw with no exceptions, not even \" and \'. That costs the
  usual way of writing a quote without quoting, and buys back a rule with
  nothing attached to it: outside of double quotes, a backslash is a byte. The
  other kind of quote is how a quote is written, which only works because runs
  concatenate. Otherwise, making all strings interpreted would lead to
  frustration when looking for a single quote or backslash. Searching for '"',
  "\"", '"', and "\'" is still viable.

- An unterminated quote is an error. The line was not finished being typed, and
  guessing where it ended would silently run half a command. Silently accepting
  it would be costlier both in implementation and headaches/confusion.

- Empty quotes produce no argument rather than an empty one. No command here
  distinguishes "" from absent, and dropping keeps 'find ""' an obvious
  "Need search" instead of an obscure empty needle. Not pure C string, so no
  null termination.

- Wildcards do not consult quoting at all, though they briefly did. They are
  matched as whole arguments before prefixes, so anything with a prefix is
  already data, and knowing how * had been quoted bought nothing but making
  find '*' an error instead of a glob. Wildcards are a product of prefixes,
  not quoting and escapes.

- Inside double quotes, a backslash covers the character after it while the
  closing quote is being looked for, before the escape itself is decoded. That
  is what lets utf8:"C:\\" work: otherwise the second backslash escapes the
  closing quote, eats its own terminator, and the argument runs to end of line
  as an unterminated quote.

- Argument does not implicitly convert to its text, and .text is a function that
  can throw rather than a field. Bytes are not text here; an alias this would
  let an argument decay into a string overload with the validation skipped, and
  a command that only ever wanted bytes should not pay for a check it did not
  ask for. utils.texts() is for unittests, where the check is the point.

- Commands look their own name up as bytes (view.prompt_command). No command is
  named something that is not text, so bytes that are not simply miss the
  lookup, and "command not found" beats complaining about the encoding of
  something that was only mistyped.

- x: is a byte string and not a number, though it used to be one. Everything
  went through parse!ulong and a byte slice of it, which made x:1122 depend on
  the host's endianness (22 11 here, 11 22 elsewhere) and x:0001 one byte
  instead of two. Both were invisible, since every test value was a palindrome.
  A hex editor's own notation is the one place where the bytes are already
  written out in order, so reading them in any other is indefensible.

- An odd digit count is an error rather than a pad. Padding is guessing which
  end gains the zero, and that guess is exactly what x: used to get wrong.

- Every scalar names a width, and there is no bare d:, o:, or 0o. Those sized
  themselves to the value, so d:255 was one byte, d:256 was two, and d:65536 was
  three, a width no scalar type has. That is the same trap x: was in, one radix
  over: the length of the needle decided by the number written in it rather than
  by what was typed. It is worse than a surprising search, because the length is
  destructive under replace and insert, where d:255 and d:256 overwrite
  different amounts of the file. Dropping them costs the two-character spelling
  and buys a rule with nothing attached: the prefix decides the length, the
  value never does.

- The width forms are named after types the program already shows rather than
  as db/dw/dd for clarity. The assembler spelling would also have collided with
  any letter suffix for endianness, back when that was still a candidate.
  Additionally, u32 and i32 spellings are more popular spellings than d32.

- 0x stayed while 0o went. They look like a matched pair of C-style aliases, but
  only one of them survives the rule above: 0x is a byte string, so its length
  is the digits, while 0o was an octal scalar with nowhere to put a width.

- Endianness is a setting and not a prefix. It is a property of the file being
  looked at, not of one needle, so the same answer applies to every scalar on
  the line and to the inspector alongside them. In the prefix it would double
  every type for something that changes once per file, and it would collide
  with the width letters besides. That is what keeps utf16: one prefix rather
  than the utf16le:/utf16be: pair every other tool carries: a code unit is a
  scalar, so it is already answered. A byte order mark is a code unit too, so
  utf16bom: is one prefix for the same reason, and the setting orders the mark
  and the text behind it together.

- A mark is a flag on the prefix, never bytes in the prefix table. The bytes it
  produces are not known until the endian setting is, so a table of literals
  would have to carry both orders of two of them and would be the endian back
  in the prefix in another spelling. The flag says a mark was asked for; the
  encoder answers with what U+FEFF is at that width and order, which is the
  same code path the text takes.

- A prefix is cheap, so "the line can already spell that" does not settle
  whether one is worth adding. It costs a row in a table and a line of
  documentation; what it buys is a thing the person typing no longer has to
  remember correctly under time pressure. The '*bom:' family is the case in
  point: every needle it produces was already spellable, and none of them were
  spellable without knowing both the mark's bytes and how the endian setting
  orders them. `find text:utf16bom utf16:hello` feels too verbose in a pinch.

- Only the i* types accept a sign at all. A minus on an unsigned type is a
  mistake worth reporting, not a wrap to perform. There is no bare i: either,
  for the same reason there is no bare u:.

- A value too large for its width is an error rather than a truncation. The
  bytes that would be searched for are not the ones that were asked for, and a
  needle that quietly matches the wrong thing is the worst outcome available.

********************************************************************************
* DOCUMENT EDITOR
********************************************************************************

To keep things simple, only the latest backend (backend/piecev3.d), will be
explained here.

All document editor backends interface IDocumentEditor (backend/base.d), and
are in charge of implementing editor features. Editors allow the editing of
IDocument (document/base.d) instances using the 'IDocumentEditor.open(IDocument)'
function.

The piecev3 backend implements a Piece Table data structure using a Red-black
self-balancing tree. For undo operations, a command-based approach is used.

A piece, simply put, contains the source material (document, buffer, etc.),
the position from the source material, its size (in Bytes), and resources
to the source material (document:file, buffer:memory data, etc.).

(See "Piece types" in the VIEWING section for details on each type)

The red-black tree helps index pieces with their cumulative size. When
searching pieces, because are already sorted by position and size, operations
are relatively fast, comparing to sorting every piece at every operation.

Example of indexed pieces:
+---------------+-------+---------------------+
| Piece         | Piece | Piece               |
+---------------+-------+---------------------+
                ^       ^                     ^
100 ------------+       |                     |
120 --------------------+                     |
220 ------------------------------------------+

When a new instance of this backend is created, a single tree instance is
created with no pieces and an empty command list.

*
* DESIGN RATIONALE
*

Why not using a memory-mapped file? (std.mmfile)
- Inefficient: Specifying size=0 loads the entire file in memory.
- Dangerous: If size is set, seeking past it truncates the file.
- Undeterministic: Depends on specific system functions. Often incompatible.

Why a Piece Table?
- Efficient: Original file data is never duplicated.
- Fast: No need to shift large amounts of data in fixed buffers.
- Natural: Changes are discrete pieces, easier to implement undo/redo.

Why a Red-Black Tree?
- Consistent: Self-balancing ensures O(log n) performance.
- Fast: Avoids performance penalties with sorting arrays.
- Efficient: Cumulative size indexing for faster address lookups.

*
* OPENING
*

When opening a document, a single document piece is inserted in the tree.

Visually, a single document piece is inserted:
+--------------------------------------+
| Document                             |
+--------------------------------------+

The piece structure instance inserted in the tree will contain (for example):
  { source=Document, position=0, size=[size of file], doc=[handle to file] }

The command history is cleared and a command entry is not created, unless we
consider opening a file as a form of edition, which is rather rare when it
comes to text editors.

*
* MODIFYING
*

When inserting or replacing data, a new piece is created and inserted into the
tree.

For each operation, some pieces will be truncated (removed and reinserted).

Example of an initial document:
+--------------------------------------+
| Document                             |
+--------------------------------------+

Example of a replace operation with a New piece:
+---------------+-----+----------------+
| Document      | New | Document       |
+---------------+-----+----------------+

Example of an insert operation with a New piece:
+---------------+-----+-----------------------+
| Document      | New | Document              |
+---------------+-----+-----------------------+

When deleting data, existing pieces will be cut and reinserted:
1. +-------------------------------+ Tree contains one piece.
   | Document                      |
   +-------------------------------+
2. +---------------+-----+---------+ Visualization of the piece to cut.
   | Document      | CUT |         |
   +---------------+-----+---------+
3. +---------------+--------+        Sliced piece, because a "gap" is created
   | Document      | Doc    |        with a new position (cut offset) and
   +---------------+--------+        new size.

In debug builds, a consistency check is performed. This check ensures pieces
have proper a size and cumulative size following each other sequentially.

When finished, a new command entry is added to the list of commands.

*
* COALESCING
*

Since the piecev3 backend, sequencial edits are coalesced as one edit
(command). This makes undoing a series of multiple edits easier.

If coalescing is enabled, and it is by default, new edits are checked
against the coalesce state. If the edit can be coalesced, the last piece is
updated with the new data.

When opening or closing a document, the coalesce state is reset.

*
* COMMANDS
*

User actions are recorded as commands in the history stack.

When an operation (insert, replace, delete) is performed, a new command is
created, executed, and added to the list of commands (history).

No snapshot of the tree is taken due to memory concerns, as the piece (v1)
backend demonstrated. Only added pieces and removed indexed pieces are saved.

The Command structure contains the minimum information to perform operations
again. For undo operations, the last operation is reversed.

Applying an operation:
1. Remove indexed pieces mentioned by the command, from the operation.
2. Adjust cumulatives by delta of all pieces after the new cumulative.
3. Add new pieces created by the opreation, with their cumulative adjusted.
4. Update the logical document size by adding delta.

Undoing an operation:
1. Remove pieces that were previously added.
2. Adjust cumulatives by -delta of pieces starting from new cumulative.
3. Add removed pieces.
4. Update logical document size by subtracting delta.

*
* VIEWING
*

When viewing the current document, the tree is traversed until the first
piece within the view range (base position and the size of the view buffer)
is located.

For each piece that needs to be read, their source material is read using the
'IDocument.readAt(long, ubyte[])' function with the base position of the piece
and the slice of the view buffer. This process is repeated until the view
buffer requested is filled, or end of the edited document is reached.

Piece types:
- source:   The original, unmodified document (typically a file on disk).
            This is the "main" source that other pieces reference.
- document: A file IDocument instance. Used when inserting content from
            another file into the current document.
- buffer:   In-memory data created during edits (inserts, replaces).
            Contains the entire data to be applied in the view buffer.
- pattern:  Special pieces for repeated data (e.g., filling with zeros).
            Pattern data is repeated as needed when filling the buffer.

*
* IN-PLACE SAVING
*

When the destination file allows to be opened as read-write, the save_inplace
function is used.

Environment variable DDHX_NO_INPLACE_SAVE=1 disables this feature.

If the size of the document has not changed since the last edit, only dirty
regions without a displacement are saved directly.

If the size has changed, then it's a whole other story. Since the document size
has changed, there is no telling which dirty regions may overlap. Writing in
the wrong order would also corrupt data before it's read.

In that case:
0. If document is larger than the target file, extend file
1. Build an array of dirty pieces with displacements.
2. Build a dependency graph for each source piece S (source).
   If another piece (P) write ranges overlaps S's read range,
   S must be written before P.
   Kahn's algorithm is used here.
3. Write pieces in topological order (back to front).
4. If document is smaller than the target file, shrink file

This should work similarly to HexFiend (BSD-2-Clause), which also inspired
this feature to be implemented. More like, Hex Friends.
